      "writtenOn": "2026-10-09T23:17:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AFAICS this will make jump_label_text_reserved() erroneously return false for a range that starts at a pKVM\u0027s static key (since `jump_entry_code(entry) + jump_entry_size(entry) \u003e (unsigned long)start` in addr_conflict() will be false)?",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a0cee47a_1082b0f7",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-11T20:46:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah and this is intentional to skip access to the pkvm.text section. This prevents decoding the size of the static branch instruction, which would cause a page fault after deprivileging because pkvm.text is already unmapped at that point.\n\nWhat I actually tried at the beginning was a bit cleaner approach and modify `kernel/jump_label.c`\u0027s   `__jump_label_text_reserved()` with\n```\n        iter \u003d iter_start;\n        while (iter \u003c iter_stop) {\n-               if (init || !jump_entry_is_init(iter)) {\n+               if (!is_pkvm_text((void *)jump_entry_code(iter)) \u0026\u0026\n+                   (init || !jump_entry_is_init(iter))) {\n```\n\nand it will work as well but then we will end-up with modifying generic non-x86 code, which I wanted to avoid.\n\nSo modifying `arch_jump_entry_size` this way (to return 0 for pkvm.text) will prevent `jump_label_text_reserved` from decoding unmapped region. And I think it should be safe since it has impact only on `addr_conflict()`, which is only used for `__jump_label_text_reserved` (which itself is used only for kprobes case anyway, and probing .pkvm.text itself is blocked by the kprobe blacklist in the next patch).\n\nBTW it is also kind of similar (though implicit) to how jump_label_text_reserved() already treats .init.text entries, but again in a way that modifies only x86 code.",
      "parentUuid": "4f784918_821efef9",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "03604da0_061eae25",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-11T21:17:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e And I think it should be safe since it has impact only on addr_conflict(), which is only used for __jump_label_text_reserved (which itself is used only for kprobes case anyway, and probing .pkvm.text itself is blocked by the kprobe blacklist in the next patch).\n\nThis is still pretty hacky and fragile, right?\n\nAnd I see there is at least one more caller of `jump_label_text_reserved()`, in `copy_optimized_instructions()` in arch/x86/kernel/kprobes/opt.c, where it is *not* gated by the blacklist?",
      "parentUuid": "a0cee47a_1082b0f7",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "62061320_e3ca2785",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-12T08:21:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This is still pretty hacky and fragile, right?\n\nIMO yes, it is a bit hacky, but only because we rely on current kernel code state where `jump_entry_size()` is only used for `addr_conflict()`/`__jump_label_text_reserved()` (and it is v. unlikely that will change in 6.18). We can solve it outside of x86 in `kernel/jump_label.c` and make it more generic but it is a tradeoff of modifying generic kernel code vs keeping it in arch/x86. Do you think that:\n```\n@@ -388,7 +393,8 @@ static int __jump_label_text_reserved(struct jump_entry *iter_start,\n \n        iter \u003d iter_start;\n        while (iter \u003c iter_stop) {\n-               if (init || !jump_entry_is_init(iter)) {\n+               if (!is_pkvm_text((void *)jump_entry_code(iter)) \u0026\u0026\n+                   (init || !jump_entry_is_init(iter))) {\n```\nwould be better?\n\n\u003e And I see there is at least one more caller of jump_label_text_reserved(), in copy_optimized_instructions() in arch/x86/kernel/kprobes/opt.c, where it is not gated by the blacklist?\n\nSorry I do not get your point here.\nThere are two different things:\n1) the next, blacklist patch, which prevents installing kprobes directly on pkvm.text itself\n2) because of the above, in this patch we are sure that any other kprobe will not overlap with a `pkvm.text` `jump_table` patch address (`pkvm.text` related static branch) because it is gated earlier by blacklist. Therefore we know that there are no `addr_conflict()` with pkvm.text, so we can safely skip checking this overlap with `pkvm.text` (which otherwise would try to decode a pkvm.text instruction to get its size).\n\nSo yes, `copy_optimized_instructions()` uses `jump_label_text_reserved()` and thanks to this patch it would be safe: decoding pkvm.text instruction will be skipped as we know that kprobe can\u0027t overlap with pkvm.text, since `copy_optimized_instructions()` is called by `arch_prepare_optimized_kprobe()` \u003c-` __register_kprobe()`, which only runs after `check_kprobe_address_safe()` (which checks `within_kprobe_blacklist()`).",
      "parentUuid": "03604da0_061eae25",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1ad95fc6_697cfc0b",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
