        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-12T23:28:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My point was that by fixing one issue we might be introducing some other, due to possible false negatives of `addr_conflict()`/`jump_label_text_reserved()` caused by the fake 0 value of size. And that in particular, even though the `within_kprobe_blacklist()` check in `check_kprobe_address_safe()` seems to prevent such false negatives for `jump_label_text_reserved()` called from `check_kprobe_address_safe()`, I\u0027m not sure it prevents them for  `jump_label_text_reserved()` called from `copy_optimized_instructions()`.\n\n\u003e copy_optimized_instructions() is called by arch_prepare_optimized_kprobe() \u003c- __register_kprobe(), which only runs after check_kprobe_address_safe() (which checks within_kprobe_blacklist()).\n\nBut `check_kprobe_address_safe()`/`within_kprobe_blacklist()` only checks a single address, while `jump_label_text_reserved()` in `copy_optimized_instructions()` is called for some _range_ of addresses, AFAICS?\n\n\u003e Do you think that: [...] would be better?\n\nNo, I think that wouldn\u0027t even pass presubmit, since `is_pkvm_text()` is defined on x86 only.\n\nAlso I guess we might have the same issue with `static_call_text_reserved()` as well, while that would limit the fix to `jump_label_text_reserved()` only.",
      "parentUuid": "62061320_e3ca2785",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "45ce7f93_fee4fb17",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-13T10:12:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e But check_kprobe_address_safe()/within_kprobe_blacklist() only checks a single address, while jump_label_text_reserved() in copy_optimized_instructions() is called for some range of addresses, AFAICS?\n\nIt is called for a range of addresses but it is limited to single function and it is looking for a 5 byte (JMP32_INSN_SIZE) range where it can install JMP32_INSN_OPCODE. So if it is about kprobing pKVM related function from pkvm.text it would be gated by second kprobe_blacklist related patch.\n\n\u003e No, I think that wouldn\u0027t even pass presubmit, since is_pkvm_text() is defined on x86 only.\n\nIt will pass the presubmit if we:\n```\n+#ifdef CONFIG_PKVM_X86\n+#include \u003casm/pkvm_image.h\u003e\n+#else\n+static inline bool is_pkvm_text(void *addr) { return false; }\n+#endif\n```\nin previous comment I\u0027ve pasted only snippets just to show idea not the whole WIP patch.\n\n\u003e Also I guess we might have the same issue with static_call_text_reserved() as well, while that would limit the fix to jump_label_text_reserved() only.\n\nNo we don\u0027t, please note that `static_call_text_reserved()` uses its own static version of `addr_conflict` (defined in kernel/static_call_inline.c), which uses `CALL_INSN_SIZE` and doesn\u0027t rely on `jump_entry_size(entry)` as `kernel/jump_label.c``s `kernel/jump_label.c`.",
      "parentUuid": "1ad95fc6_697cfc0b",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e8c01ef2_0333535c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-13T13:37:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, thanks for explaining this offline, now I see that `can_optimize()` prevents calling `copy_optimized_instructions()` etc if the range [addr; addr + JMP32_INSN_SIZE) is not within a function (and that prior `within_kprobe_blacklist()` check + your next patch ensures that if it is within a function, it is not a pKVM\u0027s function).\n\nRegarding checking `is_pkvm_text()` in `__jump_label_text_reserved()`: as we discussed offline, it seems to me that would be still hacky, as it would mean relying on some assumptions about how exactly `jump_label_text_reserved()` is used.\n\nSo perhaps this hack with returning 0 size in x86 code is still the better option short-term (until we figure out the right way to solve this)... I\u0027d just suggest to add a comment in the code to explain why we are returning 0 and why we assume it is safe to do (albeit hacky).\n\n...But I just got another idea: what if we simply give the host _read-only_ access to pKVM\u0027s text (like pKVM-ARM already does: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20250909133631.3844423-3-smostafa@google.com/)?",
      "parentUuid": "45ce7f93_fee4fb17",
      "revId": "b87c44854e168f6918d1f170349dc598695e5788",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cf2a84fe_5e01e13d",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-15T18:52:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e ...But I just got another idea: what if we simply give the host read-only access to pKVM\u0027s text (like pKVM-ARM already does: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20250909133631.3844423-3-smostafa@google.com/)?\n\nYeah, agree. As we discussed and agreed offline, this seems to be the best and easiest approach for upstreaming. I\u0027ve prepared aosp/4367474 which implements it and am going to drop this one.",
